body:
Installing a pack is the moment you decide what it may do. Before anything is
installed, Recued shows you:

- every recipe the pack will install;
- the account (the connection) it can use, if any;
- the access level it asks for, such as read, or read and write;
- the audience, which means who may use those grants. Unless you change it,
  that is only you;
- whether it may see message bodies or use local tools, if it asks for that.

The grant sticks to the installed pack. One recipe cannot quietly make it
bigger. You can change it later without installing anything again.

The ingredient names and fields below are just examples. Real ones come
from what you have installed.

```json
{
  "recipe_id": "digest-unanswered-email",
  "version": 1,
  "ttl": 600,
  "metadata": {
    "name": "Unanswered email digest",
    "description": "Summarize threads waiting on a reply from you.",
    "author": "you",
    "tags": ["email", "digest"]
  },
  "variables": {
    "days": 3
  },
  "steps": [
    { "id": "mail", "ingredient": "mail-reader",
      "input": { "folder": "inbox", "within_days": "{{config.days}}" } },
    { "id": "waiting", "transform": "filter",
      "array": "{{step.mail.messages}}",
      "field": "answered", "operator": "equal", "value": false },
    { "id": "summary", "ingredient": "ai-summarize",
      "input": { "llm.data": "{{step.waiting}}",
                 "llm.focus": "who is waiting on me" },
      "skip_when": "{{step.waiting}} is_empty" }
  ],
  "output": {
    "render": [
      { "type": "ai_analysis", "source": "step.summary" }
    ]
  }
}
```

`variables` become `{{config.*}}`. These are the values a caller fills in.
`output.render` sends step results into typed display sections. If a source
is null, that section is just not shown. Note where parameters go. Transform
parameters sit flat on the step, like `array` and `field`. Ingredient
parameters go under `input`, with dotted keys such as `llm.data`.

```json
[
  {
    "op": "task.list",
    "ingredient": "acme-catalog",
    "risk": "read",
    "approval": "never",
    "idempotency": "safe",
    "description": "List my open Acme tasks.",
    "args": [
      { "key": "query.assignee", "type": "string" }
    ],
    "bind": {
      "kind": "rest",
      "method": "GET",
      "path_template": "/v1/tasks",
      "static_query": { "limit": "100" },
      "static_headers": { "Accept": "application/json" }
    },
    "result_path": "items",
    "cache_ttl_ms": 60000
  },
  {
    "op": "task.complete",
    "ingredient": "acme-catalog",
    "risk": "write",
    "approval": "ask",
    "idempotency": "idempotent",
    "description": "Mark one Acme task complete.",
    "args": [
      { "key": "task_id", "type": "string", "required": true, "affects_target": true },
      { "key": "body.note", "type": "string" }
    ],
    "bind": {
      "kind": "rest",
      "method": "POST",
      "path_template": "/v1/tasks/{{task_id}}/complete"
    }
  }
]
```

Look at the two examples. The read takes a filter and no target. The write
takes the id of the one record it changes. And it says so.

⛔ **Unbounded requires `progress`.** An op with no deadline AND no progress
declaration has no limit at all. It runs until someone notices. Publishing
one is refused. Can you truly not watch its progress? Then declare a long
but *fixed* timeout instead.

```json
{
  "op": "media.download",
  "timeout_ms": 0,
  "bind": {
    "kind": "cli_invocation",
    "argv_template": ["yt-dlp", "-o", "{out}", "{url}"],
    "progress": {
      "contract": "heartbeat",
      "adapter": "yt-dlp-progress",
      "stall_ms": 90000
    }
  }
}
```
